 with  the blue beard fairy tale it brings up  really interesting ideas about gender  power death desire all of which exists  within silence of the lambs  well like um beauty and the beast kind  of like that  yeah beauty and the beast i mean i'm  sorry for going for like an easy one  at disney don't go into the west wing  sort of thing  sorry i mean it does have those vibes i  mean a lot of  you know it's a fairy tale that echoes  throughout so much of  western storytelling sure yeah yeah  so how responsible do you think silence  of the lambs is  for people's fascination and pop culture  with serial killers  so i think definitely silence of the  lambs was a huge  influence on the flux of serial killer  movies we ended up seeing throughout the  90s and early arts  um i think that it definitely sparked  something in hollywood  and audiences but i think american  audiences for better or for worse have  long been interested in true crime and  serial killers  we're kind of obsessed yeah i think it's  weird i'm not into  it i'm i find it both fascinating and  repulsive  there you go i think that's what most  people feel right yeah  all right and interesting yeah so  for people that like the dynamic between  clarisse and hannibal  what other characters in films would you  recommend like what other films would  you recommend that have that sort of  dynamic  so the first thing that pops into my  mind is this really great 1987  neo-noir uh called black widow that  uh follows deborah winger as a detective  who's hunting  this con woman's slash killer femme  fatale played by theresa russell  and it brings up a lot of these same  dynamics we see in silence of the lambs  which came out a few years after that  but it's two women so  the dynamics are a little different and  it's very rich and really fascinating so  sansa lambs was the last film to win  all five of the top five oscar awards is  there another film that you think  deserve  deserves that distinction ah questions  like 